---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import Cloud from "reicon/icons/Cloud";
import Rocket from "reicon/icons/Rocket";

const installCommand = "curl -fsSL https://cdn.coollabs.io/coolify/install.sh | sudo bash";
const internalUrl = "postgres://<username>:<password>@<database-container>:5432/<database>";
const backupCommand = "pg_dump --format=custom";

const faq = [
  {
    question: "Which PostgreSQL versions can I run with Coolify?",
    answer:
      "PostgreSQL 18, 17, and 16, plus Supabase PostgreSQL, PostGIS, and PGVector. The default is `postgres:18-alpine`.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I send backups to S3, Cloudflare R2, or Backblaze B2?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Any S3-compatible storage works, including Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2, and Backblaze B2.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does Coolify support point-in-time recovery for Postgres?",
    answer:
      "No. Scheduled backups are dumps, so you restore to the time a dump ran. Point-in-time recovery is continuous WAL archiving, which is a separate setup.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is my database exposed to the internet?",
    answer:
      "Not by default. A new database has no public endpoint. Apps on the same network use the internal URL.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Self-Host PostgreSQL with Automated Backups | Coolify"
  canonicalUrl="https://coolify.io/self-host-postgres-with-backups"
  description="Run PostgreSQL on your own server with Coolify. One-click setup, scheduled backups to S3, retention, and restore."
>
  <section class="px-4">
    <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-fg md:text-5xl">
      What's the easiest way to self-host a Postgres database with backups<span class="text-warning">?</span>
    </h1>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-3xl px-4 text-left">
    <Prose>
      <p class="text-base md:text-lg">
        <strong>Short answer:</strong> Install Coolify, add PostgreSQL as a one-click database, and set a backup
        schedule that uploads to S3-compatible storage. Coolify generates the password and connection URL, keeps the
        data on a volume, runs <code>pg_dump</code> on your schedule, and can restore a backup from the dashboard. You
        still own the server, its updates, and regular restore tests.
      </p>
      <p>
        Self-hosted Coolify is free and open source. One command installs it on a Linux server. The
        <a href="/deploy-apps-on-your-own-vps">VPS guide</a> has the full install steps.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="card mt-4 py-3 px-4">
      <code class="block overflow-x-auto font-mono text-sm whitespace-nowrap text-fg select-all">
        <span class="pr-2 text-fg-faint select-none">$</span>{installCommand}
      </code>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-4">
      <h2>Add PostgreSQL</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Open <strong>New Resource</strong> and pick <strong>PostgreSQL</strong>. Choose the server and Docker network.
          Build servers cannot host databases.
        </li>
        <li>
          Images: PostgreSQL 18, 17, and 16, plus Supabase PostgreSQL, PostGIS, and PGVector. The default is
          <code>postgres:18-alpine</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Coolify sets user <code>postgres</code>, database <code>postgres</code>, a generated password, a persistent
          volume, and a health check. Start it, then copy the internal URL into your app.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Apps on the same network use this URL:</p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="card mt-2 py-3 px-4">
      <code class="block overflow-x-auto font-mono text-sm whitespace-nowrap text-fg select-all">{internalUrl}</code>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-4">
      <ul>
        <li>A new database has no public endpoint. Use <strong>Public through TCP proxy</strong> or a port mapping only for an outside client, and restrict it with a firewall.</li>
        <li>Init scripts run only on an empty data directory. You can also supply <code>postgresql.conf</code>.</li>
        <li>
          For SSL, stop the database, set <strong>SSL</strong> to Enabled, and restart. <code>require</code> is the
          default. <code>verify-ca</code> and <code>verify-full</code> need the app to trust
          <code>/data/coolify/ssl/coolify-ca.crt</code>.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases/ssl">Database SSL</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases/postgresql">PostgreSQL docs page</a> lists every setting.
      </p>

      <h2>Schedule backups</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Open <strong>Backups</strong>, then <strong>+ Add</strong>. Use cron, or a named schedule such as
          <code>hourly</code>, <code>daily</code>, <code>weekly</code>, or <code>monthly</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Specific databases: <code>{backupCommand}</code>. All databases: <code>pg_dumpall</code>, gzipped.
        </li>
        <li>
          Local files go under <code>/data/coolify/backups</code>. You can limit the count, the days, and the GB.
          <code>0</code> means no limit. When any limit is reached, Coolify deletes older backups and keeps the newest.
          S3 retention uses the same three limits, set separately.
        </li>
        <li>
          Validate an S3-compatible bucket (it must already exist) and enable it on the schedule. You can delete the
          local copy after a successful upload. Guides cover Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2, Neon Object Storage, and
          Supabase Storage.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/s3-storage/overview">S3 storage</a> lists more providers, including
          Backblaze B2.
        </li>
        <li>
          The schedule uses the server timezone, or the instance timezone if the server has none. A stopped database
          skips that run. The job timeout defaults to 3600 seconds.
        </li>
        <li>
          Postgres in a one-click service works too. Coolify reads <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code>,
          <code>POSTGRES_USER</code>, and <code>POSTGRES_DB</code> from the container.
        </li>
        <li>Run <strong>Back up now</strong> and check for Success, a file size above zero, and a successful upload.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The docs say: "A successful backup only proves that Coolify created a file." Full reference:
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases/backups">Database backups</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Restore a backup</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Stop application writes and take a separate backup of the target first.</li>
        <li>
          Open <strong>Import Backup</strong> on the running database. The file can come from your computer, an absolute
          path on the server, or S3.
        </li>
        <li>
          Coolify picks <code>pg_restore</code> or <code>psql</code> and shows the command. It accepts SQL, custom, tar,
          and all-database dumps, compressed with gzip, bz2, xz, or zip. The restore runs in one transaction. Replacing
          existing objects, and keeping owners and privileges, are off by default.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can also import through the API. See
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases/restore">Restore a database</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What you still own</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>OS updates, the firewall, and SSH, on self-hosted Coolify and on Coolify Cloud.</li>
        <li>Restore tests. A backup file does not prove your app can read the data.</li>
        <li>An off-server copy. Local backups sit on the same server as the database.</li>
        <li>Major upgrades. Follow PostgreSQL's upgrade steps, and take a verified backup first.</li>
        <li>Database data itself. A Coolify instance backup saves settings, not your database contents.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When a dump is the wrong tool</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          You need point-in-time recovery. These backups are dumps, so you restore to the time a dump ran. PostgreSQL
          documents point-in-time recovery as
          <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html">continuous WAL archiving</a>, which
          is a separate setup.
        </li>
        <li>You need several database nodes. The one-click database is a single container.</li>
        <li>
          You do not want to run a server. In both self-hosted Coolify and Coolify Cloud, the database runs on a server
          you provide. A managed Postgres provider is the alternative.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Deploying the app that uses this database:
        <a href="/deploy-apps-on-your-own-vps">deploy apps on your own VPS</a>. Comparing platforms:
        <a href="/compare/coolify-vs-dokploy-vs-caprover-vs-dokku-vs-kamal">Coolify vs Dokploy vs CapRover vs Dokku vs Kamal</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <Faq items={faq} />
    </div>

    <section class="card mt-14 p-6 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-fg">Get started<span class="text-warning">.</span></h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-xl text-sm text-fg-dim">
        Install Coolify and deploy your first database. If you would rather not run the dashboard, Coolify Cloud hosts
        it. Your databases stay on your servers.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/deploy-your-first-database" class="btn btn-primary">
          <Icon icon={Rocket} class="size-4" />
          Deploy your first database
        </a>
        <a href="/pricing" class="btn btn-neutral">
          <Icon icon={Cloud} class="size-4" />
          Try Coolify Cloud
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
